## Not affiliated with 1Password

Kestrel is an independent project. It is not produced, endorsed, sponsored or
supported by AgileBits Inc., and it is not affiliated with or connected to
1Password in any way.

"1Password" is a trademark of AgileBits Inc. The name appears in this repository
only to describe interoperability: Kestrel's importer and exporter read and
write the 1PUX archive format, which is a published, documented file format.
That support exists so a person can move their own data in and out; it is not a
claim of association and it does not imply any endorsement. The importer maps
the category identifiers that appear in an exported 1PUX file onto Kestrel's own
categories; those identifiers are part of the archive format, not of any
implementation.

No 1Password code, assets, artwork, icons, fonts, translation strings or other
copyrighted material is contained in this repository or in a Kestrel build. The
implementation is original work, written against public specifications:
Argon2id (RFC 9106), HKDF-SHA256 (RFC 5869), XChaCha20-Poly1305
(draft-irtf-cfrg-xchacha), TOTP (RFC 6238), SQLite, WebAuthn/CTAP2 and the
native-messaging protocol of the major browsers.

## Third-party components

Kestrel depends on third-party open-source software, each under its own licence.
The authoritative list of versions is `src-tauri/Cargo.lock` for the Rust
workspace and `package-lock.json` for the renderer; the licences themselves come
from each package's own manifest (`cargo metadata`, `npm ls --json`), which is
also what `cargo deny check licenses` reads. Every dependency is used under the
terms of its own licence; those terms are not restated here and are not replaced
or modified by the MIT licence of this project.

Fonts: Inter (SIL Open Font License 1.1) is vendored as a build asset under its
own licence. Cascadia Mono is named in the monospace stack and is not shipped:
the renderer falls back to whatever monospace the platform provides.
Icons: Lucide (ISC licence), consumed through `lucide-react`.

## Security disclosure

Kestrel stores secrets and is used by people who cannot afford a silent bug.
Please report suspected vulnerabilities privately to the maintainers rather than
in a public issue tracker.
